[h1]Speculative Markets[/h1]
Speculative Markets adds financial booms, bank lending and market crashes to Victoria 3. Investors follow rising prices, banks lend into the boom, and things can get ugly when prices turn. Your laws, credit policy and decisions affect how far it goes and who ends up paying for it.

Every country also gets an Exchange, with its own charter, specialities and place in the world rankings.

Built and tested on [b]Victoria 3 1.15.1[/b]. You can add it to a running campaign or continue a save from an earlier version of the mod.

New changes go through [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839275304]Speculative Markets - Beta[/url] before coming here. [b]Use either stable or Beta, never both.[/b]

[h2]Getting started[/h2]
Click [b]Finance[/b] on the left sidebar. The panel has four tabs: Exchange, Speculation, Banking and Rankings. The tooltips explain the numbers and what is affecting them.

[h2]Exchange[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/uU0sfan.png[/img]
Your Exchange starts at Provincial standing and can grow into a National, International or World Centre. Higher standing unlocks more Specialities.

Choose a charter to give it a focus: a [b]Commodity Exchange[/b] encourages stronger booms, a [b]Money Market[/b] favours steadier trust, and a [b]Share Market[/b] gives you more credit. The Paths to Renown let you compete with other Exchanges in your chosen Specialities, with better bonuses for higher placements.

[h2]Speculation[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/NiNIlhY.png[/img]
[b]Market Confidence[/b] runs from 0 to 100. Booms raise it; crashes, debt and lost wars bring it down. Above 80, investors become reckless. Below your Panic Threshold, a Financial Panic starts.

During a panic, output and tax revenue fall, banks call in loans, and private construction is cut in half. The treasury also has to cover relief for the unemployed.

Each good has its own [b]Investor Interest[/b], ranging from Fleeing to Feverish. Goods attracting more interest get more investment, but a bigger boom can mean a worse crash. You can restrain speculation in a good, encourage investment where it has dried up, or float a Great Issue to increase your Exchange score while taking on more risk.

[h2]Banking[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/8ob11sb.png[/img]
Credit helps your economy grow, but too much makes a panic harder to recover from. Each month of [b]Credit Leverage[/b] adds 2% throughput, up to 24%. Beyond 3 months, the Panic Threshold rises and panics leave Bad Debts that reduce throughput and investment for years.

The [b]Lending Limit[/b] starts at 3 months of investment pool income. Finance technologies and a leading Exchange can raise it to 12. Set your credit policy to Tight, Normal or Loose to control how freely banks lend.

If a panic hits, [b]Lend to the Banks[/b] shortens it and reduces the output, tax and private construction penalties. Relief payments still continue. You can buy up bad loans afterwards, or make a pledge beforehand to rescue the banks automatically at a lower cost.

[h2]Rankings[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/kfYbzHP.png[/img]
Compare every Exchange at National standing or above. The top five receive prestige, cheaper loans and a higher Lending Limit.

You can also pick a [b]Rival Exchange[/b] with a score at least as high as yours. At the end of each year, the one with the higher score takes trust from the other.

[h2]Other features[/h2]
[list]
[*]A Great Power's panic can spread through trade and foreign ownership. From around 1870, it can develop into a worldwide Long Depression.
[*]The Financial Markets journal entry opens a map showing market conditions in each country. Stripes mark countries a panic has recently spread to.
[*]Economic system and trade policy laws affect the markets. Their tooltips list the effects. Speculation is disabled under Command Economy or Cooperative Ownership.
[*]Alerts below the top bar warn you about approaching panics and speculative bubbles.
[*]The Market Volatility game rule has a High setting if you want more volatile markets.
[/list]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
[list]
[*]Using CMF? Add the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839275348]CMF patch[/url] and load it last.
[*]Using Morgenroete? Add the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839705018]Morgenroete patch[/url]. Disable the old Speculative Markets+Morgenroete patch.
[/list]
With both, use this order: [b]CMF > Morgenroete > Speculative Markets > Morgenroete patch > CMF patch.[/b] You can use Beta in place of Speculative Markets. Don't enable both.

Mods that replace the sidebar, goods panel or building definitions may need a patch.

For multiplayer, everyone needs the mod.

Available in English, Brazilian Portuguese, French, German, Japanese, Korean, Polish, Russian, Simplified Chinese, Spanish and Turkish. Translations from English are machine translated. Please let me know if something reads badly.

[h2]Feedback[/h2]
Let me know how it plays, especially if panics seem too frequent or crashes feel too punishing. Reports from longer campaigns are useful for balancing it.

If you find a bug, include your game and mod versions, the other mods you're using, and what happened. A screenshot or error log helps. You can post in the comments or the bug-report discussion.

